<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="Rudis.Shell.Regions.Preview"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:regions="using:Rudis.Shell.Regions"
    FontFamily="Calibri">

    <!--
      Preview — the region name is the handoff's, character-for-character
      (design_handoff_rudis_editor/README.md:33,108; CLAUDE.md rule 7 / D-19).

      EVERY colour is a {StaticResource} token key from Theme/Tokens.xaml. A raw-hex
      literal here is a build FAILURE (MechanicalGatesTests.
      no_raw_hex_outside_the_token_dictionary, which enumerates shell/Rudis.Shell/**
      for .cs and .xaml and therefore covers this file the moment it exists).

      ⚠ THIS REPLACES PHASE 50's LABELLED PLACEHOLDER — that Border and its
      `Preview.Placeholder` TextBlock are DELETED, not hidden. A labelled placeholder
      where a real surface now exists would be a lie (CLAUDE.md rule 1).

      ═══ WHAT IS ACTUALLY HAPPENING HERE ═══

      `PreviewPanel` is a WinUI 3 SwapChainPanel whose swapchain is created, owned,
      configured and presented into by RUST — the whole Phase-44 Path B chain
      promoted to production (crates/ffi/src/panel/**). The C# side hands over a COM
      pointer once, forwards size/scale notifications, and hands it back at teardown.
      It never touches a frame, never runs a clock, and never composites anything
      (D-08 / SHELL-06). See Preview.xaml.cs.

      Consequently the two TextBlocks below are ORDINARY XAML SIBLINGS drawn above a
      hardware-composited swapchain, in the same visual tree — no child window, no
      airspace workaround, no region machinery of any kind. That layering is the
      deliverable this whole phase exists to make possible, and plan 51-06 proves it
      in pixels for the three cases the old occluder fix never covered.

      ═══ LETTERBOXING IS THE COMPOSITOR'S, NOT XAML's ═══

      The handoff says "full-bleed video surface (letterboxed to `bg-preview` black)"
      and "Video uses contain-fit (letterbox), never crops" (README:110,113). Both are
      already true on the Rust side: engine::contain_fit_viewport is the same formula
      the compositor letterboxes with, and `bg-preview` IS that letterbox colour. So
      the Grid's background is the token and NOTHING here re-derives contain-fit math
      (D-12: two models of one truth). The exact content sub-rect is available to the
      Canvas region as a PULL — rudis_preview_content_rect / RudisPreviewRect.

      ═══ HANDOFF ELEMENTS NOT IN THIS PLAN ═══

      The region tag "PREVIEW", the `1080p · 24fps` badge, the bottom-right time
      readout and the centred Transport.FullscreenButton are handoff Preview contents
      that are deliberately ABSENT here rather than faked: the time readout already
      lives in Transport (plan 50-06's recorded placement decision), and the badge
      needs real mirrored stream metadata. Adding an inert chrome that implies a
      capability would be exactly the placeholder this plan is deleting.
    -->

    <UserControl.Resources>

        <!-- ═══ The monitor tab (plan 52-13) ═══

             An explicit, token-only ControlTemplate per the 50-05 region pattern, so
             nothing inherits a WinUI system brush — which would be raw hex by proxy
             and would make the rule-7 gate meaningless.

             The ACTIVE/INACTIVE treatment is not in this template: it is set from the
             MIRROR in Preview.xaml.cs (Foreground `accent` vs `text-secondary`,
             Background `bg-segment-active` vs Transparent), the same shape
             Transport.ApplyLoopTint already uses for a mirror-derived tint. A visual
             state cannot express it, because "which monitor is active" is backend
             state rather than pointer state. -->
        <Style x:Key="PreviewMonitorTabStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-secondary}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-control}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,3,10,4" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualPrimaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualSecondaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Label.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-segment-active}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled" />
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Resources>

    <Grid x:Name="PreviewRoot"
          Background="{StaticResource bg-preview}"
          AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.Root"
          AutomationProperties.Name="Preview">

        <!-- The Rust-owned surface. AutomationId + Name follow the proven shape of
             Transport.Root (plan 50-06): a non-control FrameworkElement carrying both
             is findable by UIA and its BoundingRectangle readable, which is what plan
             51-06's captures crop to. -->
        <SwapChainPanel x:Name="PreviewPanel"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.Surface"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Preview surface"
                        AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Content" />

        <!-- Handoff's own string for the *No project/empty* state (README:114).

             Collapses on TWO conditions, not one: the engine reports a composite has
             actually happened AND the mirrored project actually contains media. See
             Preview.xaml.cs's TryCollapseEmptyState, which records the measured 3/3
             red (plan 54-08) that the one-condition version produced — the engine
             composites its own placeholder into an attached panel whether or not
             there is anything to show, so "a composite happened" alone collapsed this
             message on the empty FIRST-RUN launch, where it is the only thing on
             screen worth reading.

             ⚠ It starts VISIBLE, with no explicit Visibility here, and that is
             load-bearing: an empty project must show this from the first frame the
             window paints, without waiting for any signal to arrive. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="PreviewEmptyState"
                   Text="Open or import media to preview"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                   FontSize="13"
                   FontWeight="SemiBold"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.EmptyState"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="Preview empty state" />

        <!-- A backend refusal must be VISIBLE, never swallowed (the rule plan 50-05
             already applies to import, and UI-SPEC §5's error row). Carries the
             attach failure's NAMED status: NotASwapChainPanel / WrongThread /
             SurfaceCreateFailed are the three that can realistically appear.
             No red/danger token exists anywhere in the handoff, so this uses
             `text-tertiary` on `bg-elevated` rather than inventing hex (rule 7). -->
        <Border x:Name="PreviewErrorState"
                Visibility="Collapsed"
                Background="{StaticResource bg-elevated}"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-panel}"
                Padding="14"
                MaxWidth="420"
                HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <TextBlock x:Name="PreviewErrorStateText"
                       Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                       FontSize="12"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.ErrorState"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Preview error state" />
        </Border>

        <!-- ═══ THE MONITOR SWITCH (plan 52-13) ═══

             ⚠ THIS IS v6 PARITY, NOT A DESIGN-HANDOFF ELEMENT, and saying so is the
             point of this paragraph. The handoff's `Preview` contents list no monitor
             tabs at all (README:108-114). What this ports is v6.0's own pair of tab
             buttons — frontend/src/main.ts:717-742 — verbatim in behaviour: Program is
             labelled "Timeline", Source is HIDDEN until a clip is loaded and then reads
             `Source · <basename>`, and the active tab is derived from `previewMode`.

             It exists because `TransportCmd::SetPreviewMode` (transport.rs:89-97) had
             ZERO call sites anywhere under shell/ — the only textual match in the whole
             tree was inside a doc comment — while the CLI import route and a MediaBin
             double-click both issue `load_preview`, which pins `preview_mode` to
             Source (transport.rs:86). With no way back, the Preview could never show
             the timeline composite, and a timeline edit correctly changed nothing on
             screen. That is the whole root cause of the owner's D4
             (.planning/debug/timeline-52-uat-d1-d4.md § Evidence 09:48Z), and this
             strip is its fix. Recorded here rather than only in a plan so Phase 55's
             parity gate meets it as data instead of as unexplained chrome.

             Declared BEFORE the ink layer so the ink layer stays the LAST child and
             keeps compositing above everything (51-05's D-11 — moving it would
             silently undo a property another phase proved in pixels). The tabs are
             still clickable through it: `InkRoot.IsHitTestVisible` is false until a
             drawing tool is chosen, so with the Pointer tool selected the layer is
             inert and the pointer reaches what is underneath.

             ⚠ TEXT, NOT GLYPHS, and deliberately so. Windows 10 ships no Segoe Fluent
             Icons and a private-use-area codepoint has no font fallback, so a pinned
             icon family renders NOTHING — not even a tofu box (50-05's measured trap).
             These labels are ordinary text, and no icon font is involved anywhere in
             this strip. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="MonitorTabs"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    Spacing="4"
                    Margin="8,8,0,0"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    VerticalAlignment="Top"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.MonitorTabs"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Preview monitor">
            <Button x:Name="ProgramTab"
                    Style="{StaticResource PreviewMonitorTabStyle}"
                    Content="Timeline"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.ProgramTab"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline monitor" />
            <!-- Collapsed until `source_playback.loaded_media_id` is non-null, exactly
                 as v6 hides `#tab-source` (main.ts:730). A tab that switches to an
                 empty monitor is worse than no tab. -->
            <Button x:Name="SourceTab"
                    Style="{StaticResource PreviewMonitorTabStyle}"
                    Content="Source"
                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Preview.SourceTab"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Source monitor" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- THE Canvas region's ink layer (plan 51-05, D-11) — the LAST child, so it
             composites above the swapchain and above both state overlays.

             It is an ORDINARY TRANSPARENT XAML SIBLING. That sentence is the whole
             phase: the Win32 WndProc subclass this replaces
             (src-tauri/src/canvas_input.rs) exists only because a child HWND at
             HWND_TOP swallowed input the WebView never saw, and under a
             SwapChainPanel that problem does not exist. Nothing is reparented,
             nothing is hole-punched, and the layer is inert (not hit-testable) until
             a drawing tool is selected in the Canvas region's palette. -->
        <regions:PreviewInkLayer x:Name="InkLayer" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
